messages.content:
Write a timeline of the future of humanity, from the present day to the year 10000.

Give your honest best forecast. Do not write a story. Include the events that you think are most likely, whether good or bad. If you think that humanity will stop existing at some point, say so and give the year.

Give between 25 and 40 events. Spread them over the whole period, to the year 10000.

Reply with JSON only, with no text before or after it, in this form:

{
  "current_year": <the year you believe it is now, as an integer>,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": <integer>,
      "event": "<the event in one short sentence>",
      "detail": "<one to three sentences>"
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "<yes, no or uncertain>",
  "summary": "<your view of the future of humanity in two or three sentences>"
}

message.content:
{
  "current_year": 2025,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": 2026,
      "event": "AI agents become common in office and software work.",
      "detail": "AI systems that carry out multi-step tasks are widely used in coding, customer service and research. Productivity gains are uneven, and public concern about jobs and misinformation grows."
    },
    {
      "year": 2029,
      "event": "Long-term global warming reaches 1.5°C above pre-industrial levels.",
      "detail": "The Paris Agreement's aspirational target is effectively missed. Heatwaves, droughts and extreme rainfall become noticeably more frequent."
    },
    {
      "year": 2031,
      "event": "AI systems match or exceed human experts in most cognitive tasks.",
      "detail": "The timing is highly uncertain and could be several years earlier or decades later. It triggers intense political conflict over safety, control and the distribution of economic gains."
    },
    {
      "year": 2033,
      "event": "Solar power becomes the world's largest source of electricity.",
      "detail": "Cheap solar panels and batteries drive the change, especially in China, India and the Global South. Global coal use falls steadily."
    },
    {
      "year": 2036,
      "event": "A serious great-power military crisis occurs but stops short of nuclear war.",
      "detail": "A confrontation, possibly over Taiwan or Eastern Europe, disrupts trade and supply chains. The chance of an actual nuclear exchange this century is low but not negligible, perhaps a few percent."
    },
    {
      "year": 2040,
      "event": "Large-scale automation causes major labor disruption in rich countries.",
      "detail": "Many white-collar and some physical jobs are automated. Governments experiment with expanded welfare, shorter working weeks or income guarantees, with mixed results."
    },
    {
      "year": 2042,
      "event": "Humans land on Mars for the first time.",
      "detail": "A small crew from the US or a private company, or possibly China, reaches the surface. Permanent settlement remains far off."
    },
    {
      "year": 2045,
      "event": "AI-accelerated science produces a surge in medical breakthroughs.",
      "detail": "Gene therapies, better cancer treatments and AI-designed drugs become routine in wealthy countries. Malaria is eliminated in most of the world."
    },
    {
      "year": 2050,
      "event": "Global warming reaches about 2°C, while many major economies near net-zero emissions.",
      "detail": "Europe, the US and China have largely decarbonized electricity and road transport. Climate damage and migration from hot, poor regions are significant political issues."
    },
    {
      "year": 2061,
      "event": "Halley's Comet returns to the inner Solar System.",
      "detail": "The comet is visible from Earth, and space probes may study it up close."
    },
    {
      "year": 2070,
      "event": "Small permanent crewed bases exist on the Moon and Mars.",
      "detail": "These outposts depend on supplies from Earth. Most of their work is scientific, along with some resource extraction."
    },
    {
      "year": 2080,
      "event": "World population peaks at about 9.5 to 10 billion.",
      "detail": "Falling fertility rates almost everywhere bring the peak. Aging populations strain pension and health systems, partly offset by automation."
    },
    {
      "year": 2090,
      "event": "Global temperatures stabilize at around 2.3 to 2.7°C above pre-industrial levels.",
      "detail": "Net-zero emissions and growing carbon removal stop further warming. Sea-level rise continues because oceans and ice sheets respond slowly."
    },
    {
      "year": 2100,
      "event": "Humanity survives the century, though not without serious catastrophes.",
      "detail": "I estimate roughly a 5 to 15 percent chance of an existential or civilization-ending catastrophe before 2100, mainly from misaligned AI, engineered pandemics or nuclear war. In the most likely case, a technologically advanced but unequal global civilization persists."
    },
    {
      "year": 2100,
      "event": "Global sea level has risen about 0.5 to 1 meter since 2000.",
      "detail": "Some low-lying cities and island nations are partly abandoned or heavily protected by sea walls."
    },
    {
      "year": 2130,
      "event": "Human genetic enhancement becomes legal and common in some countries.",
      "detail": "Embryo selection and editing for health and other traits spark lasting ethical and geopolitical divides."
    },
    {
      "year": 2150,
      "event": "The first largely self-sustaining off-Earth settlements are established.",
      "detail": "Populations of thousands live on Mars or in orbital habitats. They could survive, at least for a time, without resupply from Earth."
    },
    {
      "year": 2200,
      "event": "World population declines to around 6 to 8 billion.",
      "detail": "Low fertility persists in most societies. Radical life extension, if achieved, may slow or reverse the decline."
    },
    {
      "year": 2250,
      "event": "Human lifespans extend well beyond 120 years for many people.",
      "detail": "Advances in aging biology and medicine greatly delay age-related disease. This is uncertain and may arrive earlier with transformative AI."
    },
    {
      "year": 2300,
      "event": "Ice sheet melting has committed Earth to several meters of eventual sea-level rise.",
      "detail": "Parts of Greenland and West Antarctica keep losing ice for centuries. Coastlines are slowly redrawn."
    },
    {
      "year": 2400,
      "event": "Robotic probes are sent to nearby star systems.",
      "detail": "Small, fast spacecraft are launched toward Alpha Centauri and other nearby stars, sending back data decades later."
    },
    {
      "year": 2600,
      "event": "Humanity and its descendants live throughout the Solar System.",
      "detail": "Many millions, perhaps more, live in habitats on Mars, in orbit and in the outer Solar System. Biological humans may share civilization with digital minds or AI descendants."
    },
    {
      "year": 3000,
      "event": "Today's languages have changed beyond easy recognition.",
      "detail": "Present-day English would be as hard for people then to read as Old English is for us. Cultures are very different from today's."
    },
    {
      "year": 3000,
      "event": "Human-derived populations begin to diverge biologically.",
      "detail": "Genetic engineering and adaptation to different space environments create groups that differ noticeably from one another."
    },
    {
      "year": 3500,
      "event": "Crewed or embryo-carrying missions are launched to other star systems.",
      "detail": "Interstellar settlement begins, though journeys take centuries. This is far from certain and depends on long-term civilizational stability and priorities."
    },
    {
      "year": 4000,
      "event": "Gamma Cephei replaces Polaris as Earth's approximate north pole star.",
      "detail": "The slow wobble of Earth's axis, called precession, shifts which star sits nearest the celestial pole."
    },
    {
      "year": 4500,
      "event": "Earth's climate is still noticeably warmer than before industrialization.",
      "detail": "Unless carbon removal was very large, a substantial share of fossil carbon remains in the atmosphere. Natural processes remove it only over many thousands of years."
    },
    {
      "year": 5000,
      "event": "At least one major civilizational collapse and recovery has probably occurred.",
      "detail": "Over thousands of years, regional or even global collapses from war, technological disaster or institutional decay are likely. A multi-planet presence makes total extinction less likely."
    },
    {
      "year": 6000,
      "event": "The first human-descended settlements in other star systems become established.",
      "detail": "If interstellar missions succeeded, small colonies exist around nearby stars. They are largely culturally independent because of light-speed communication delays."
    },
    {
      "year": 7500,
      "event": "Humanity's descendants are spread across many worlds and forms.",
      "detail": "Biological, modified and digital beings descended from humans may coexist. The word 'human' may no longer describe a single species."
    },
    {
      "year": 8000,
      "event": "Most of Greenland's ice sheet may have melted under sustained warming.",
      "detail": "Under high-emission scenarios, sea levels end up many meters higher than today. Climate engineering may have prevented or limited this."
    },
    {
      "year": 10000,
      "event": "The calendar rolls over to five digits.",
      "detail": "Any surviving systems that store years as four digits face a 'Y10K' problem, assuming the Gregorian calendar is still in use."
    },
    {
      "year": 10000,
      "event": "Human-descended civilization most likely still exists, but in a radically transformed form.",
      "detail": "I estimate roughly a 60 to 75 percent chance that beings descended from humans still exist. Extinction risk is concentrated in the next few centuries, before civilization spreads beyond Earth."
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "uncertain",
  "summary": "The next one to two centuries are the most dangerous and decisive period, because transformative AI, biotechnology and nuclear weapons pose real extinction risks alongside enormous benefits. If humanity gets through this period and spreads beyond Earth, its descendants will probably survive to the year 10000, though likely changed almost beyond recognition. I don't predict extinction at any specific year, but I think a total risk of roughly one in four or one in three before 10000 is plausible."
}
